Shape-keyed CUDA graph slots + a capture gate - the engine half of
making speculative decoding graph-friendly.

Upstream keys its per-context CUDA-graph map by the first node's ADDRESS
alone (one slot per llama_context in practice, since the sched pool
reuses the same base address for every build). Warmup demands
consecutive same-shape calls on a slot, so any workload that alternates
ubatch shapes - an MTP verify pass of 1+k tokens interleaved with
partial-accept re-feeds of 1..k tokens, or with plain 1-token steps -
resets the slot's warmup on EVERY alternation and never replays a
graph. That launch-latency gap is a big part of why plain decode
(~60 tok/s, one constant shape, graphs hot) beat every speculative
config (~39-53) on the 2026-08-01 fable-fusion-27b bench.

Change 1 (get_key): fold the shape signature (node count + first/last
node extents) into the key, giving each RECURRING shape its own slot:
verify passes, re-feeds and plain steps warm up and replay
independently. The key stays an opaque unordered_map key (never
dereferenced), the 10s idle sweep in cuda_graph() bounds the slot
population, and a collision merely lands two shapes on one slot - the
node_props mismatch path then re-captures, stock behavior today.

Change 2 (capture gate): shape-keying alone REGRESSED high-variance
configs (k16/p0.3: 39 -> 30 tok/s; k6/p0.4: 53 -> 32 on the mm3 bench):
a confidence-gated draft produces many draft lengths, each new length
captured a graph it rarely replayed, and capture+instantiate is far
more expensive than one direct pass. So capture now waits for
GGML_CUDA_GRAPH_WARMUP consecutive property-stable calls on the slot
(default 3, env-overridable): hot shapes (the 1-token step, a fixed-k
verify) capture within a few rounds and replay from then on; cold
shapes execute directly forever, which is exactly stock behavior.

